43
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:22.856
<v Daniel>And that is exactly the problem REE solves. Evaluating open-ended research is notoriously messy. REE strips away all that subjective human bias by grading iterative improvement against a hard, automated metric.</v>

44
00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:27.498
<v Maya>Okay, so let's break down this exam because the specs on this are just brutal.</v>

45
00:04:27.718 --> 00:04:28.259
<v Daniel>They really are.</v>

46
00:04:28.519 --> 00:04:35.145
<v Maya>It's 100 open-ended research problems drawn from active research, all reviewed by human domain experts.</v>

47
00:04:35.325 --> 00:04:39.890
<v Daniel>And the breadth is wild. It spans five broad areas in 23 different domains.</v>

48
00:04:40.517 --> 00:04:49.182
<v Maya>Yeah. Everything from mathematics and computing to physical sciences, earth sciences. It even covers economics, finance, and the science of science itself.</v>

49
00:04:49.462 --> 00:05:01.128
<v Daniel>Right. And for every single problem, the setup is identical. The agent gets a problem statement, a working starter solution, which is just some basic code that functions but performs badly, and some development data.</v>

50
00:05:01.805 --> 00:05:12.232
<v Maya>And then they get exactly 12 hours offline. Yes. Which is such a tough constraint. Plus, they restrict the compute budget, right? They only give it four CPUs and 16 gigabytes of RAM.</v>

51
00:05:12.473 --> 00:05:15.054
<v Daniel>Which in the AI world is basically a toaster.</v>

52
00:05:15.295 --> 00:05:21.559
<v Maya>Right. It forces them to actually be clever instead of just brute forcing a million simulations.</v>

53
00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:28.484
<v Daniel>Exactly. And then it scores them on a 0 to 100 scale on held out test data that they never get to see.</v>

54
00:05:29.032 --> 00:05:30.913
<v Maya>So they can't cheat by memorizing the answers. Right.</v>

55
00:05:31.353 --> 00:05:36.074
<v Daniel>Zero is the starter solution, and 100 is the absolute metric upper bound.</v>

56
00:05:36.815 --> 00:05:43.557
<v Maya>But the most crucial design choice here, the thing that makes this paper so fascinating, is what they grade the AI against.</v>

57
00:05:43.777 --> 00:05:45.017
<v Daniel>The expert baseline.</v>

58
00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:53.120
<v Maya>Yes. They don't just grade it in a vacuum. For every problem, there's an established expert solution, like a published method or a Kaggle winner.</v>

59
00:05:53.430 --> 00:06:03.076
<v Daniel>But they don't just take the score from the original paper. The authors actually squeeze this human-made expert solution into the exact same 12-hour, four CPU constraint to create a true baseline.</v>

60
00:06:03.677 --> 00:06:12.402
<v Maya>So it's like taking a Michelin star chef's signature dish, but forcing them to cook it in a dorm room using only a microwave and a mini fridge?</v>

61
00:06:12.802 --> 00:06:14.744
<v Daniel>That is a perfect way to put it, yeah.</v>

62
00:06:15.204 --> 00:06:21.488
<v Maya>You're leveling the playing field, but the chef's dish might not taste as good as it did in their actual restaurant.</v>

63
00:06:21.783 --> 00:06:28.585
<v Daniel>And the authors completely acknowledge that. The expert methods sometimes score lower here than in their original papers because of those constraints.</v>

64
00:06:29.385 --> 00:06:37.348
<v Maya>Okay, so we've built up the difficulty of this exam. Let's get to the headline numbers. Did the agents actually pass?</v>

65
00:06:38.088 --> 00:06:46.951
<v Daniel>Well, let's start with the baseline. When you force the human expert solutions into that dorm room setup, they average a score of 24.4 out of 100. Okay, 24.4 is the bar. How did</v>

66
